<https://w3id.org/project-chemie-digital/graph/scenes/level5-lognormal> {
  ex:l5-l12a-scene a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:lognormal-concentration-model ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l5-l12a-heading, ex:l5-l12a-text, ex:l5-l12a-plot ; cd:narration "The dilution story introduced concentration changes by factors. Products of positive factors become sums after taking logarithms; this motivates examining a lognormal candidate, but an arbitrary product is not automatically lognormal. We now use twenty new deliberately constructed concentrations to illustrate that model and the upcoming log transformation. They are not field measurements and not the forty site values from N11, and they are not a fit to those values. Seven log-spaced levels have frequencies one, two, four, six, four, two, one; ties are intentional. The specified reference has median 2 mg/L and log-ratio SD 0.75. Its positive support and long right tail express the candidate model, not evidence that our environmental story establishes it. Histogram areas sum to one and theoretical curve coordinates are connected approximately. The zero-density endpoint is a limiting value, not an observed zero or point mass; the tail continues above 10 mg/L, with about 1.594% probability beyond that boundary. Keep these same twenty values for the next step: take their log ratios to show precisely how the lognormal model connects to a normal one."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-l12b-scene a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:lognormal-concentration-model ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l5-l12b-heading, ex:l5-l12b-plot, ex:l5-l12b-math ; cd:narration "L12b. Transform exactly the same twenty values, without resampling, dropping observations or removing ties. Every transformed observation links to its original concentration. To avoid taking a logarithm of a dimensional quantity, divide concentration by a fixed reference of 1 mg/L and take the natural logarithm of that dimensionless ratio. A different reporting unit requires converting the reference too; it does not change the ratio. Negative log ratios are allowed and mean a positive concentration below the reference, not a negative concentration. The graph is a histogram of transformed values, not merely a logarithmic set of ticks on the original concentration-density plot. Its bin widths and density heights are on a new, dimensionless scale, so heights cannot be compared directly with the previous slide; area remains the sample fraction. The specified normal reference has mean ln(2), about 0.693147, and standard deviation 0.75 on the log-ratio scale. Its second normal parameter is variance. These parameters are not the arithmetic mean and standard deviation of concentration. The deliberately symmetric transformed illustration makes the relation visible but establishes no fit to real data; a logarithm does not generally make arbitrary skewed data normal. The displayed normal curve has finite drawing limits but extends over the full real log-ratio axis."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-l12c-scene a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:lognormal-concentration-model ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l5-l12c-heading, ex:l5-l12c-plot, ex:l5-l12c-math ; cd:narration "L12c. Return to the original concentration scale and keep the specified lognormal population, not the empirical histogram. Symmetry on the log-ratio scale places the median at the reference concentration times exp(m). The population geometric mean has the same value when the log expectation exists; here normality guarantees that existence. Both equal 2 mg/L. The arithmetic expectation instead includes half the log variance inside the exponential and is about 2.6495695 mg/L, rounded to 2.65 mg/L. The upper tail pulls the arithmetic mean above the median. The curve markers locate those concentration summaries; their vertical positions are density heights, not probabilities and not alternative definitions of the summaries. The parameters used numerically are m = ln(2) and s = 0.75; displayed m = 0.69315 is rounded. The twenty constructed sample values have arithmetic mean about 2.589815 mg/L and geometric mean 2 mg/L, so population expectation and sample mean must not be confused. Exponentiating an arithmetic mean of observed log ratios and multiplying by the reference gives the sample geometric mean, not generally the sample arithmetic mean. The expectation formula here is exact under the lognormal population model, not a universal correction for every fitted log-scale regression or arbitrary data transformation."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-l12d-scene a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:lognormal-concentration-model ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l5-l12d-heading, ex:l5-l12d-list ; cd:narration "L12d. Check support, reporting and mechanism before taking logarithms. A true zero is not in a lognormal population and has no finite natural logarithm. Adding an arbitrary constant changes the variable and does not justify a lognormal model for the original concentration. A zero-containing process may require a different or mixed model; that choice depends on the process, not on making the software accept a log. Results below detection or quantification limits are censored information, not known exact zeros. Missing observations are different again. Preserve reporting limits and use a justified analysis rather than silently deleting or replacing values. Mixtures of populations, censoring and dependent observations can create apparent skewness or invalidate a simple model. A log transform is a candidate analytical representation, not a guarantee of normality. Back-transformation must retain the intended target: a geometric mean is not an arithmetic mean. No real environmental dataset or formal goodness-of-fit result has been established here. Later sections will distinguish a distribution of individual concentrations from a distribution of sample means; this four-slide increment does not introduce that sampling simulation yet."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
